---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = '/misc/spring-break-2026/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Hoodoos, Earthships and Red Rocks — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Spring break 2026: a four-day driving loop from the Arizona line to the Bisti badlands, the Taos Earthships, Red Rocks, an all-night run to St. George and Las Vegas, with our dog Wangcai.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · March 2026</p>
  <h1>Hoodoos, Earthships and Red Rocks</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Spring break 2026: a four-day loop through the Southwest and into the Rockies, with Wangcai along for the ride.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · March 19–22, 2026</strong><br />
    The California line at Topock → I-40 across Arizona → the Bisti/De-Na-Zin badlands → Albuquerque →
    Santa Fe → the Taos Earthships → Pueblo → Denver → Red Rocks → I-70 into the Rockies → Vail → overnight to St. George, Utah →
    Las Vegas → home<br />
    Six states: California, Arizona, New Mexico, Colorado, Utah and Nevada ·
    one badlands at sunset · one off-grid town built from tires · one empty amphitheatre · one very large cream soda · one cream-colored dog
  </div>

  <p>
    Spring break is less a vacation than a gap in the calendar, and this one was four days long. We spent it the way you spend a gap you don't want to waste: in the car. The plan was a loop, west to
    east and back again, that would string together four very different places. There was a badlands in
    northwestern New Mexico, a town of houses made of tires near Taos, a concert venue carved into red sandstone
    outside Denver, and, on the way home, the glass conservatory at the Bellagio. In between were a great many miles of
    interstate.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the whole loop: from Topock on the California-Arizona line east across Arizona to the Bisti badlands, south to Albuquerque, north to Taos and Denver, west on I-70 to St. George, Utah, and on to Las Vegas." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    As with the last trip, everything here is reconstructed from the camera roll. A phone keeps a time and a place for
    every picture, and four days of them turn out to make a decent diary. It is an honest one too: it shows where we
    stopped to look, and it goes quiet where we just drove. There are some long silences in this one. Where the photos
    say nothing, so do I.
  </p>
  <p>
    The loop also had a passenger who doesn't appear in the stats: <strong>Wangcai</strong>, our cream-colored dog,
    who came the whole way. Her name means, roughly, "flourishing fortune", and it is about as classic a dog name as there is in
    Chinese, the equivalent of calling a dog Rover. She does not care about any of that. She has her own section
    further down, mostly involving dirt.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Across Arizona to the badlands</h2>
  <p>
    My first photo of the trip was taken at 6:40 in the morning at <strong>Topock</strong>, where I-40 crosses the
    Colorado River from California into Arizona. It is a passenger-window shot: the guardrail, a desert shrub, a power
    line and a sky going from blue to pale peach over low, dark hills, with the side mirror in the corner. Dawn at the state line meant an overnight start:
    we had left home the night before and slept in the car at a Supercharger station near the California–Arizona line.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'topock-dawn.webp'} alt="View from a car window at dawn: a highway guardrail, desert shrubs and low hills under a pale peach sky, with the side mirror in the corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dawn at the Arizona line, 6:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there it is the better part of a day across Arizona on I-40, the interstate that replaced most of old Route
    66. It runs past Kingman and Flagstaff, up onto the high plateau and through the ponderosa forest, and then out
    across the long, open stretch past the Painted Desert. My own camera didn't record much of it, which is
    honest: there is a lot of Arizona, and most of it is the same color for a long time. My wife's has a morning stop
    in Kingman, and a break in Williams outside the <strong>Grand Canyon Brewery</strong>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'grand-canyon-brewery.webp'} alt="A red barn-roofed wooden building with a Grand Canyon Brewery sign and grain silos beside it, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Grand Canyon Brewery in Williams, 9:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0319.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0319.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1, from Topock across Arizona on I-40, into New Mexico, and north on NM-371 to the Bisti/De-Na-Zin Wilderness." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: from the California line to the Bisti badlands, then on to Albuquerque for the night.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    My camera woke up again around 1:20 in the afternoon at Lupton, the last town before New Mexico, with a few short
    videos out the windshield. Crossing the line costs you an hour: Arizona doesn't observe daylight saving time and
    New Mexico does, so in March the clock jumps forward the moment you cross. Just past Gallup, at Thoreau, we turned
    north onto <strong>NM-371</strong>, a two-lane road that runs straight up across the Navajo Nation toward
    Farmington, through open rangeland with mesas on the horizon. Then, about an hour later, came a turn onto a
    county gravel road and a dirt parking lot with nothing else in it but a few cars and an information board.
  </p>

  <h3>The Bisti/De-Na-Zin Wilderness</h3>
  <p>
    The board said <strong>Bisti/De-Na-Zin Wilderness</strong>. It is a protected wilderness of about 45,000 acres,
    run by the Bureau of Land Management, and it is one of the strangest landscapes in the Southwest. The names are
    Navajo. <em>Bisti</em> comes from a word meaning "among the adobe formations", and <em>De-Na-Zin</em> means
    "standing cranes", after petroglyphs of cranes found nearby. There is no visitor center, no water, no shade and
    no marked trail. You park, you pick a direction, and you walk out across a flat, gray, crunchy plain toward
    whatever looks interesting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first interesting thing was color. Half an hour out, a pair of rounded hills rose from the gray flats, their
    tops a rusty red and their sides streaked with cream and charcoal. The light was already low and warm at 5:30, and
    the sky above them was the hard, saturated blue of the high desert, a blue that gets deeper the longer you look at it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-red-hills.webp'} alt="Two rounded hills with rust-red tops and pale streaked sides rising from a flat gray-brown plain under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first red hills, half an hour out from the trailhead.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    What you are walking on is the floor of a seventy-million-year-old coast. In the Late Cretaceous, this corner of
    New Mexico sat at the edge of the Western Interior Seaway, a shallow sea that split North America in two. Rivers
    wound through swamps and floodplains on their way to it, and laid down layer after layer of sand, mud and peat.
    The sand became sandstone, the mud became shale and mudstone, and the peat became the thin black bands of coal you
    can see in the hillsides. Dinosaurs lived in those swamps, and their bones still turn up here. Then the whole
    region was lifted up, and the wind and the occasional violent rain have been taking it apart ever since.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-bisti.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-bisti.webp'} alt="Map of the walk: from the trailhead out past the red hills to the cracked eggs, slabs, banded mound and hoodoo field, and back to the trailhead." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The walk: a little over two hours, out along the Bisti Wash and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    That erosion is what makes Bisti famous, because it doesn't wear everything down evenly. Hard layers resist and
    soft layers don't, and the result is a landscape of objects. Out along the wash, the ground was scattered with
    the formations people call the <strong>cracked eggs</strong>: rounded lumps of harder rock, each with a pattern of
    fine curving lines, lying in clusters on the gray clay like the shells of something that had hatched and left. A
    little further on, a whole field of pale stone slabs lay split and stacked, as if a wall had been knocked over and
    left where it fell.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-eggs.webp'} alt="A cluster of rounded, finely layered rock lumps lying on gray clay in low sunlight, casting long shadows." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cracked eggs.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-slabs.webp'} alt="A line of pale broken sandstone slabs and blocks lying on a flat gray plain, with low badlands on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sandstone slabs, broken where the ground beneath them washed away.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then came the hoodoos. A hoodoo is a pillar of soft rock wearing a cap of hard rock, like a mushroom or a
    toadstool. The cap shields the column underneath from rain, while everything around it erodes away. At Bisti
    they come in every size, from knee-high to taller than a person, and in places they stand in crowds. The best field
    we found was a jumble of white and cream mushrooms, with wide flat caps balanced on fluted, wind-carved stems. In
    the late light, every one of them threw a long blue shadow across the sand. It looked like a sculpture garden that
    nobody had designed, which is exactly what it is.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-hoodoo-caps.webp'} alt="Close view of pale hoodoos with wide flat caps on fluted stems, with more of them scattered across the gray badlands behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Flat caps on fluted stems.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-hoodoos.webp'} alt="A crowded field of white mushroom-shaped hoodoos with flat caps and fluted stems, casting long shadows in late sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A hoodoo field at 6:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Out there, a long way from the road, there is no traffic, no water and nothing built by people in any direction,
    and the loudest sound is usually your own boots on the crust of the clay, which is dry and crunchy underfoot. The hills are striped like a cut cake: a cap of rust red, a band of
    bright white, a thick layer of charcoal-gray clay below. One mound, half in shadow at a quarter past six, showed
    the whole sequence at once. Further off, a long wall of pale, wind-worn rock stood alone on the plain, like the
    ruin of a castle.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-banded.webp'} alt="Two badland mounds banded red, white and charcoal gray, half in shadow, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The layer cake: red, white and charcoal.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-outcrop.webp'} alt="A long, pale, eroded rock outcrop standing alone at the far side of a flat brown plain." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A lone outcrop across the flats.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By 6:17 the sun was sliding down toward the western rim, and from the top of a low rise we looked straight into it,
    across a dark bowl of eroded clay to the flat horizon. There are a dozen photos from that one spot, taken within a
    minute and a half, and then a few more minutes there with Wangcai. The light came in so flat and low that the badlands went almost
    black, with just a silver edge on every ridge, and the sun made a white star in a sky streaked with contrails.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bisti-sunstar.webp'} alt="The low sun as a bright star over a dark, eroded badlands bowl, with contrails across a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the Bisti Wash, 6:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Wangcai discovers the badlands</h3>
  <p>
    Wangcai did the whole walk with us, out across the clay and back.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-bisti.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a yellow bandana sitting on gray clay among small hoodoos, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai among the hoodoos, 5:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    But her real
    contribution to the geology of the San Juan Basin came at the very end, back at the trailhead, just after
    seven. The sun was nearly gone, and the last of it came in sideways across the gravel lot, turning everything it
    touched to gold, including her. She considered the situation, lay down in the dirt, and rolled. Then she rolled
    again. She stretched out with all four legs pointing at the horizon, upside down, her yellow bandana askew, and
    gave us the expression of a dog who has found the single best patch of ground in New Mexico. A water bowl
    stood next to her, ignored.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-roll-1.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a pink leash lying on her side on gravel in golden evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Phase one.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-roll-2.webp'} alt="The same dog lying on her back in the dirt, legs in the air, lit gold by the setting sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Phase two.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Phase three was stillness: flat out on her side in the last stripe of sunlight, eyes closed, mouth open in what can
    only be described as a grin, coated in a fine layer of Cretaceous dust.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-roll-3.webp'} alt="The dog stretched out in the gravel with her eyes closed and mouth open, a water bowl beside her." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Complete satisfaction.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is hard to say who had the better evening. We had all walked for a little over two hours through one of the most
    otherworldly places in the country. We came back with photos of hoodoos; she came back and found the one warm patch
    of gravel that made the whole day worth it. On the evidence of these photos, it was a tie. Then it got dark, the badlands went from gold to gray to nothing, and
    we drove off into the night, south to Albuquerque, where we slept. How much of the San Juan Basin came along on
    Wangcai's coat isn't in the record.
  </p>
  <p>
    We got there in time for a late dinner at the <strong>Frontier</strong>, the diner across from the university:
    an enchilada plate in red chile, a bowl of posole, a bowl of green chile stew and a big flour tortilla. Across the
    street, a long wall of colored glass was lit from inside.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'abq-frontier.webp'} alt="A tray with an enchilada plate smothered in red chile and cheese, a bowl of red posole, a bowl of green chile stew and a folded flour tortilla." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Late dinner at the Frontier.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'abq-glass-wall.webp'} alt="A long, low building with a wall of multicolored glass panes lit from inside at night, with a flowering tree and a lawn in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A wall of colored glass, across the street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Day 2: Red or green, then the Earthships</h2>
  <p>
    The next photo was taken at 11:42 the following morning, in <strong>Albuquerque</strong>, on the edge of Old Town.
    That is the original heart of the city: a plaza and an adobe church founded under Spanish rule in 1706, a full
    century and a half before most of the American West had towns at all. We were there, at least on the evidence of
    the camera, for brunch.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0320.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0320.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2, from Old Town Albuquerque north to the Earthships west of Taos, then north on NM-522." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Albuquerque, the Earthships, and north into the dark. Santa Fe and Pueblo aren't on the map.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    New Mexico has an official state question, adopted by its legislature in 1996: "Red or green?" It refers to chile,
    which here is not a spice so much as a food group, and it is asked about almost everything on a menu. Red chile is
    made from ripe pods that are dried and ground; green is roasted from the fresh ones. The correct answer, if you
    can't decide, is "Christmas", meaning both. Our table came out Christmas: a burrito smothered in dark red chile on
    one side and green on the other, buried in melted cheese, with a plate of fried eggs and hash browns beside
    it and small bowls of sides. After brunch, an Old Town courtyard had tulips out among the prickly pear.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'abq-christmas.webp'} alt="A smothered burrito covered in red and green chile and melted cheese, next to a plate of fried eggs and hash browns, on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Christmas", in Old Town Albuquerque.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'abq-old-town.webp'} alt="An Old Town courtyard with adobe walls, brick paving, potted tulips and a prickly pear cactus under bare trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An Old Town courtyard.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The Earthships</h3>
  <p>
    From Albuquerque we drove up to <strong>Santa Fe</strong> for the afternoon to buy gifts and souvenirs, and I
    forgot to take a single photo there. Luckily my wife didn't: she caught the dining room at <strong>La Fonda</strong>
    on the Plaza, a courtyard roofed with skylights, with trees growing up through it and panes of hand-painted glass.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'santa-fe-la-fonda.webp'} alt="A skylit hotel dining room with trees growing up through it, wooden tables and chairs, and doors of hand-painted glass panes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>La Fonda on the Plaza, Santa Fe.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    My camera roll picks up again at 7:18 in the evening, on a sagebrush mesa
    northwest of <strong>Taos</strong>. To get there you cross the Rio Grande Gorge on US-64, on a steel bridge
    that hangs about 650 feet above the river. Out on the far side, on a plain that looks too open and empty for
    anyone to live on, sits one of the oddest neighborhoods in America: the <strong>Greater World Earthship
    Community</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    An Earthship is a house designed to need nothing from the outside world. The idea belongs to Michael Reynolds, an
    architect who started building them in the desert around Taos in the 1970s, out of what other people throw away.
    Each house heats and cools itself with the sun and its own mass, catches its own water from the roof, makes its own
    electricity, and treats its own sewage. From a distance, at sunset, they look like something between an adobe
    pueblo and a moon base: curving earth walls, round towers, green-painted parapets, wind turbines and solar panels,
    all half-sunk into the ground among the sagebrush, with the Sangre de Cristo Mountains blue behind them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-mesa.webp'} alt="Low earth-sheltered houses with solar panels and a wind turbine among sagebrush, with the Sangre de Cristo Mountains under a pink sunset sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The community at sunset, under the Sangre de Cristos.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-door.webp'} alt="A green, curving earth-walled building with an arched wooden door outlined in tile, a small wind turbine beside it, at sunset." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An Earthship doorway, with its own wind turbine.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Up close, at the visitors center, you can see how they're made. The heavy outer walls are old car tires, laid like
    bricks and rammed full of earth with a sledgehammer until each one is as hard as stone and weighs several hundred
    pounds. Stacked in courses and plastered over with mud, they make walls so massive that they soak up the day's
    heat and give it back slowly through the cold desert night. The lighter walls are built of glass bottles set in
    adobe like stones in concrete. From outside you see a polka-dot pattern of dark bottle bases in the cracked mud,
    and from inside, when the sun is behind them, they glow like stained glass.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-tires.webp'} alt="A curving wall built of stacked earth-filled car tires at the base, with a mud wall above studded with dark glass bottle ends." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rammed tires below, bottles above.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-bottles.webp'} alt="Close-up of a cracked mud wall studded with round dark bottle bases, with colored bottles and stones along the top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bottle wall, up close.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The buildings themselves are part house, part sculpture. One near the visitors center is a stack of drums: a fat
    concrete cylinder, a slimmer tower wrapped in a spiral of golden bottle-ends, and a sloping wall of bottles behind,
    with scaffolding still on one side and solar panels angled on top. Another is a long, steep greenhouse of glass,
    facing south with its back to the north wind, which is the basic Earthship plan. The glass front catches the
    winter sun, and the earth-bermed back keeps the heat in. As the sky went yellow behind it, it looked like a
    spacecraft that had landed in the sagebrush and decided to stay.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-tower.webp'} alt="A round concrete Earthship building with a tower spiraled with golden bottle ends, solar panels and wooden scaffolding, in evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A tower of drums and bottles at the visitors center.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-greenhouse.webp'} alt="A steep, glass-fronted Earthship greenhouse topped with solar panels, silhouetted against a yellow dusk sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A south-facing glass front, at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Seen from the road at a distance, the community is mostly roofline: humps of earth, round domes and little
    sculpted turrets poking out of the brush, with glinting panels between them. People really do live here. These are
    homes, with trucks parked outside, and some of them are rented to visitors who want to try off-grid life for a night.
    The sagebrush grows right up to the walls, and in the low light the whole place takes on its color: a dusty sage
    green, with mountains behind.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-roofline.webp'} alt="Earth-sheltered Earthship buildings with sculpted parapets, domes and solar panels rising out of sagebrush, with mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Earthship rooflines rising out of the sagebrush.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Not every resident was human. Behind a fence in one of the yards, a pale dog stood in the sagebrush with its head
    thrown back, howling at the evening. It wasn't ours, though it could have passed for a cousin.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'earthship-local-dog.webp'} alt="A pale dog standing in sagebrush in front of an Earthship at dusk, head raised, howling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A local, saying goodnight.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By 7:48 we were back on the road, heading north on <strong>NM-522</strong> past Arroyo Hondo toward the Colorado
    line. The last photo of the day is the sky over the road. A thin crescent moon hung in the deep blue, with a single
    bright planet below it and a band of orange afterglow along the black edge of the hills. It's the kind of sky
    you only get far from cities, and a good one to drive into. It was a long way yet: dinner was at a Texas Roadhouse
    in Pueblo, and we slept in Denver.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'nm522-moon.webp'} alt="A thin crescent moon and a bright planet in a deep blue dusk sky above an orange afterglow and black hills." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The crescent moon over NM-522, 7:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Denver, Red Rocks and the road west</h2>
  <p>
    The next morning we were in <strong>Denver</strong>, which for me was a return visit. I'd last been here in the
    summer of 2024 for SIGGRAPH, and had spent an evening walking through the Civic Center and the museum district at
    sunset. This time we were back at the same corner on a bright March morning, with a sky full of rippled
    high cloud.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0321.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0321.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3, from the Denver Art Museum and the Yearling to Red Rocks Amphitheatre, then west on I-70 into Clear Creek County." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: Denver, Red Rocks, then west on I-70. The walk to Union Station and dinner in Vail aren't on the map.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The <strong>Denver Art Museum's</strong> Hamilton Building was exactly as I remembered it: Daniel Libeskind's
    titanium shard, all tilted planes and points, leaning out over the plaza as if it were about to take off. Across
    the way, outside the Central Library, <em>The Yearling</em> was finally out from behind the construction fence that
    had hidden it two summers ago. Donald Lipski's painted pony stands on top of a red chair about twenty feet tall,
    and in the bare March trees, with nothing in the way, you could appreciate how absurd and how charming it is.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-art-museum.webp'} alt="The angular, titanium-clad Hamilton Building of the Denver Art Museum leaning over a wide paved plaza under a rippled cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Denver Art Museum's Hamilton Building.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-yearling.webp'} alt="A sculpture of a brown-and-white pony standing on a giant red chair on a lawn outside a stone library building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Yearling</em>, no fence this time.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the museum plaza, the view north runs across Civic Center Park to the downtown towers. Parts of the
    park were fenced off for work that morning, but the skyline was clean and sharp under the high cloud.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-civic-center.webp'} alt="Looking north from a paved plaza across Civic Center Park, with a fenced colonnade and the downtown Denver towers under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Denver from the museum plaza.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Outside the museum, Claes Oldenburg and Coosje van Bruggen's <em>Big Sweep</em>, a giant broom and dustpan, leans
    against the Hamilton Building, and Wangcai posed on the plaza in her yellow bandana.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-big-sweep.webp'} alt="A giant sculpture of a broom and a blue dustpan against the angular titanium wall of the Denver Art Museum." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Big Sweep</em>, at the art museum.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-denver.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a yellow bandana sitting on a paved plaza in front of the angular Hamilton Building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai at the Denver Art Museum.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the museum we walked past the gold dome of the <strong>Colorado State Capitol</strong> across Civic Center
    Park, and on to the Convention Center, where a giant blue bear presses its nose to the glass.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-capitol.webp'} alt="The gold dome of the Colorado State Capitol above bare trees across Civic Center Park." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Capitol dome, across Civic Center Park.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-blue-bear.webp'} alt="A giant blue bear sculpture peering into the glass wall of the Colorado Convention Center, with downtown towers beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The big blue bear at the Convention Center.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The walk ended at <strong>Union Station</strong>, where the train hall is a row of white canopy arches over the tracks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-union-station.webp'} alt="Denver Union Station's train hall: white arched canopies over empty platforms and tracks, with a Track 4 sign, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The train hall at Union Station.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Red Rocks</h3>
  <p>
    Fifteen miles west of downtown, the Great Plains stop and the Rockies begin, and right at the seam is
    <strong>Red Rocks Park</strong>. The rocks are great fins of red sandstone, tilted up out of the earth at a steep
    angle when the mountains rose. They belong to the Fountain Formation, laid down about three hundred million years
    ago from the debris of a far older range. It is the same rock that makes the Flatirons above Boulder and Garden of
    the Gods near Colorado Springs. From the top of the park you look east between the fins, past a cone-shaped
    hill and a reservoir, to the plains running flat to the horizon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-fins.webp'} alt="Tilted fins of red sandstone with pines and scrub, looking east over hills and a reservoir toward the plains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Red sandstone fins, looking east.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-valley.webp'} alt="Red sandstone steps and railings in the foreground, with a conical hill, a reservoir and the plains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from the steps.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The real reason everyone comes, though, is the <strong>amphitheatre</strong>. Between two enormous monoliths,
    Ship Rock and Creation Rock, each about three hundred feet tall, the slope has been shaped into about 9,500 seats
    looking down at a stage, with the plains and, on a clear day, Denver laid out behind it. It was built in the late
    1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, the New Deal program that put young men to work on public lands, to a
    design by the Denver architect Burnham Hoyt, and it opened in 1941. Musicians love it for the acoustics, which the
    rocks provide for free. The Beatles played here in 1964, and U2 filmed a famous concert here in the rain in 1983.
  </p>
  <p>
    In March there were no concerts, and the bowl was nearly empty. A few people sat scattered across the long
    wooden benches in the sun, while the afternoon shadow of the rim crept slowly down the rows. It is a huge space,
    and with no one in it you notice how steep it is, and how the rows sweep in long, curved lines down to the stage like
    contour lines on a map.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-bowl.webp'} alt="The nearly empty Red Rocks Amphitheatre seen from high up, rows of benches sloping down to the stage between red rock walls, with the plains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Red Rocks Amphitheatre, between shows.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At the top of the seating is a plaza where, on show nights, the crowds buy their drinks and T-shirts. That
    afternoon the stalls were shuttered, but the strings of bulbs were still there, slung
    across the plaza and up toward the rock, which rose straight out of the paving, layered and fractured, three hundred
    feet into a sky streaked with cloud. The bulbs only made it look bigger.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-ship-rock.webp'} alt="A massive monolith of layered red sandstone rising above an empty plaza, with strings of light bulbs slung across the deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One of the two monoliths, from the top plaza.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Wangcai, it turns out, makes an excellent concertgoer. Leashed dogs are welcome when there's no show, and she
    settled onto a concrete step between two benches in the high rows, facing the stage like she was waiting for the
    opening act. In some of the photos she's watching something off to the side, ears up, with the stage, the red rock
    walls and the plains spread out behind her. In others she simply lies there in the sun, looking pleased with the
    acoustics.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-redrocks.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a yellow bandana lying on a step high in the Red Rocks seating, with the stage and red rocks below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, in the good seats.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the top of the park, the view east reaches all the way to Denver. The downtown towers stand in a small, hazy
    cluster on the horizon, past the foothills and the suburbs, with the red rock in the foreground. Behind us, the
    road wound back down between the fins toward the parking lots. At four in the afternoon, under a sky of streaming,
    feathery cloud, we followed it down and out.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-denver.webp'} alt="Downtown Denver's towers small on the horizon beyond foothills and suburbs, seen over red rocks from Red Rocks Park." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Denver, fifteen miles away.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'redrocks-road.webp'} alt="A road curving between tilted red sandstone fins and brown hills under a sky of wispy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The road out, past the fins.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>I-70 into the Rockies</h3>
  <p>
    A quarter of an hour later we were on <strong>I-70</strong> heading west, and the whole Front Range came into view at
    once through the windshield: a row of white peaks above the dark, forested foothills, with a 65 mph sign at the
    side of the road. I-70 through the Colorado mountains is one of the great feats of American highway engineering.
    It climbs from the plains to more than eleven thousand feet at the Eisenhower Tunnel, the highest point on the
    entire Interstate system, threading canyons that look far too narrow for a four-lane road.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i70-front-range.webp'} alt="An empty interstate stretching west toward a range of snow-capped mountains, with a 65 mph speed limit sign and pines at the roadside." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Front Range from I-70, 4:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Half an hour later, past Georgetown and up the valley of Clear Creek near Bakerville, we were in real winter.
    The slopes on both sides were white, with dark firs standing in the snow and avalanche chutes running straight down
    the mountainsides. It was spring on the plains, but up here it was still winter, and the road kept climbing toward
    the tunnel and the ski country beyond.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i70-snow.webp'} alt="Snow-covered mountains and dark forested slopes beside I-70, with traffic ahead under a dramatic cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Clear Creek County, near Bakerville.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i70-forest.webp'} alt="A steep mountainside of snow and dark evergreens, with an avalanche chute running down the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Snow and firs above the highway, 4:55 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And there my camera roll goes quiet. The photo of that snowy slope, at 4:55 pm, is my last one for almost
    twenty-two hours, and not because nothing happened. We kept going: over the Divide to Vail, where we had dinner at
    Pepi's, an Austrian restaurant (Wiener schnitzel with red cabbage, and apple strudel in vanilla sauce), then down the far side of the Rockies, across the whole width of Utah in the dark, and down to <strong>St. George</strong> in the state's
    southwest corner, where we pulled in at five in the morning. That is a full night's drive after a full day, and
    none of it was photographed. I'd love to tell you about it, but the phone was apparently as tired as we were.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vail-schnitzel.webp'} alt="A Wiener schnitzel with two lemon wedges, carrots, potatoes and red cabbage, with a second plate of stew and spätzle behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wiener schnitzel at Pepi's, Vail.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vail-strudel.webp'} alt="A slice of apple strudel dusted with sugar, with vanilla sauce, whipped cream and a scoop of ice cream on a white plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Apple strudel in vanilla sauce.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Day 4: St. George and Las Vegas</h2>
  <p>
    We slept until about eleven, which after a night like that felt like an achievement, checked out, and bought a
    Swig cream soda of genuinely alarming size for the road. Then it was down I-15 through the Virgin River Gorge and
    across the corner of Arizona into Nevada. Our car is electric, so Las Vegas was a natural place to stop and
    recharge it, and while it charged we went to look around.
  </p>
  <p>
    The record resumes at about half past two on Sunday afternoon on the <strong>Las Vegas Strip</strong>, with pastries
    at Caesars Palace, and the contrast with the day before could hardly be sharper. The afternoon before, we had been looking at snow, firs and mountain highway; now
    we were in the warm desert sun, shoulder to shoulder with what looked like half of Nevada, inside the
    <strong>Bellagio Conservatory</strong> next door.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0322.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0322.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4, from St. George, Utah, down I-15 to the Bellagio Conservatory and the fountains lake on the Las Vegas Strip." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: from St. George down to the Strip.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The conservatory is a great glass-roofed hall, with iron arches and a cream-and-gold Beaux-Arts arcade around the
    edges. It has been there since the Bellagio opened in 1998, and its gardeners replant the whole thing several
    times a year, for Lunar New Year, spring, summer, autumn and the winter holidays. It is free to walk through, which
    is why it is always packed. We caught the spring show. Giant yellow tulips on stems taller than a person arched over
    the paths, beds of real flowers filled the floor, and enormous balls covered in
    roses hung from the ceiling like ornaments.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-tulips.webp'} alt="Giant yellow glass tulips, a hanging rose-covered ball and a sculpted bird under the arched glass roof of the Bellagio Conservatory." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Spring in the Bellagio Conservatory.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The theme seemed to be birds and eggs. Huge sculpted songbirds, yellow and black like goldfinches, swooped
    overhead between giant butterflies, and a gilded birdcage stood in the middle of the beds. Two decorated eggs taller
    than a person, one lilac and one pink and studded with jewels like Fabergé eggs at the wrong scale, stood at the far
    end among clipped topiary. The noise in the hall was a constant warm roar of voices, and every few seconds
    someone held up a phone to take exactly the picture we were taking.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-birds.webp'} alt="Giant sculpted songbirds and butterflies flying beneath the glass roof of the conservatory, with a hanging flower ball and a gilded birdcage." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Goldfinches the size of a sofa.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-eggs.webp'} alt="Two giant jeweled decorative eggs, one lilac and one pink, among topiary and hanging flower balls in the conservatory." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fabergé, at the wrong scale.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Outside, Bellagio Drive curves down past the lake toward the Strip. Through the pine branches you could see the whole
    of it at once: the water where the famous fountains dance on schedule, a skyline of casino
    towers and cranes, and on the right, the half-scale Eiffel Tower of Paris Las Vegas, pointing at a perfectly blue
    desert sky. It was 3:28 pm, and it is my last photo of the trip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-eiffel.webp'} alt="The Las Vegas Strip seen through pine branches: the Bellagio lake, casino towers, and the Paris Las Vegas Eiffel Tower against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Strip from Bellagio Drive, my last photo of the trip.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We had hot chicken at Hattie B's before leaving, and by about a quarter to six, with the car charged, we were on
    the drive home to Southern California, which has hardly any photos either.
  </p>

  <h2>Four days, four landscapes</h2>
  <p>
    Looking back over the camera roll, what strikes me is how little the four big stops have to do with each other. The
    first was a place the earth made by itself, slowly, out of an ancient swamp: no buildings, no trail, nothing
    but rock and the evening light. The second was a place people made, deliberately, out of what everyone else threw
    away, to see whether they could live without the grid. The third was a place where people took something the
    earth made and shaped it, just enough, into a room for twenty thousand ears. And the fourth was a building that
    imports the whole idea of spring, by the truckload, into the middle of a desert, and replants it five times a
    year.
  </p>
  <p>
    The badlands, an off-grid town, a sandstone concert hall and a glass garden on the Strip: that is a strange
    sequence for one long weekend, and the driving in between was most of the trip. But the in-between is the point
    of a loop like this. You see the country change outside the windshield: Mojave scrub, then the high plateau,
    then sagebrush, then red rock, then snow, then the desert again. Every few hours you get out and stand somewhere
    you've never been. Four days, well over two thousand miles, and a dog who was happiest of all in a gravel parking lot at
    sunset. No regrets.
  </p>
